### Criteria for and consequences of presenting a counterexample

We relate how one can refine a counterexample and use it to obtain more
restrictive hypotheses. In particular, if we have an equation f^2(m) * f^{f(m)}(m) = m^2, any
compl种(f m) differences from m would necessarily imply by conference of this equation
that f m = m. We refine this to not only show that f m = m if f^2(m) * f^{f(m)}(m) = m^2 and
f m ≠ 0, but also if one is zero, it implies that the product is zero, so m must be zero. -/

